<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
    <head>
        <!--include head.txt -->
        <title>
            Statistical Fallacies
        </title>
    </head>

    <body>
<!--include menu.txt -->
        <h1>
            Statistical Fallacies
        </h1>

        <details>
            <summary class="sum1">
            Availability Bias
            </summary>
            <p>
            We often judge how common or likely something is by how easily
            examples of it come to mind. Psychologists Amos Tversky and
            Daniel Kahneman called this the <b>availability heuristic</b>.
            It is a handy shortcut, but it misleads us whenever some events
            are much easier to remember than others.
            </p>

            <details>
                <summary class="sum2">
                    Examples
                </summary>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                    After a plane crash is in the news for a week, many
                    people become more afraid of flying, and some drive
                    instead, even though the drive is far more dangerous
                    per mile traveled. The crash is vivid and
                    memorable; the thousands of uneventful flights each day
                    never make the news.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                    Lottery winners are interviewed on TV; the millions of
                    people who bought losing tickets are not. So winning
                    "feels" much more likely than the 1 in 14 million odds we
                    computed on the
                    <a href="Possibilities.html">Possibilities</a> page.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                    Ask people whether more English words <em>start</em>
                    with the letter K or have K as their <em>third</em>
                    letter. Most say "start with K," because those words are
                    easier to call to mind. In fact, words with K in the
                    third position are more common.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary class="sum2">
                    Why it is a statistical error
                </summary>
                <p>
                The examples that come to mind are a <em>sample</em>, but
                they are not a
                <a href="Sampling.html">random sample</a>.
                They are chosen by what is dramatic, recent, or
                emotionally striking, and that is a biased way to sample.
                <br />
                <b>The remedy:</b> before deciding that something is common
                or rare, ask what the actual counts or rates are, rather
                than relying on what you happen to remember.
                </p>
            </details>

            <p>
            <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Availability_heuristic">
                The availability heuristic on Wikipedia.
            </a>
            </p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary class="sum1">
            The Gambler's Fallacy
            </summary>
            <p>
                <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gambler%27s_fallacy">
                The Gambler's Fallacy
                </a>
                <br>
                <br>
                <a
                    href="https://www.caesars.com/casino-gaming-blog/latest-posts/table-games/roulette/gambling-myth-monte-carlo-fallacy">
                    The Monte Carlo Fallacy</a>
    
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary class="sum1">
            Ignoring Fat Tails
            </summary>
            <p>
                <a href="https://gcallah.github.io/statistics/FatTails.html">
                    Fat Tails</a>
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary class="sum1">
            Discarding unfavorable data
            </summary>
            <p>
            A conclusion can only be as good as the data behind it. If the
            data that would have argued <em>against</em> a conclusion are
            left out, even honest arithmetic on what remains will mislead.
            This can be done on purpose ("cherry picking"), or it can happen
            without anyone intending it.
            </p>

            <details>
                <summary class="sum2">
                    Cherry picking
                </summary>
                <p>
                Reporting only the results that support your case.
                <br />
                <b>Example:</b> A fund manager advertises "up 40% over the
                last three years," choosing a starting date right at a
                market low. Starting the chart a year earlier, at the peak,
                might show a loss.
                <br />
                <b>Example:</b> A researcher removes a few data points as
                "outliers," but only the ones that weaken the result.
                </p>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary class="sum2">
                    Survivorship bias
                </summary>
                <figure>
                    <img width="330"
                    src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Survivorship-bias.svg/500px-Survivorship-bias.svg.png"
                    alt="A bomber with red dots marking where returning planes had been hit">
                    <figcaption>
                        Damage on the bombers that made it back.
                        (Image: Martin Grandjean, McGeddon, US Air Force;
                        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>)
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p>
                Sometimes the unfavorable data are discarded by the world
                before we ever see them: we only get to look at the cases
                that "survived."
                <br />
                <b>Example:</b> In World War II, the military studied the
                bullet holes on bombers returning from missions, and
                considered adding armor where the holes were most common.
                The statistician Abraham Wald pointed out that these were
                the planes that <em>made it back</em>. The places with no
                holes, such as the engines, were where hits brought a plane
                down. That is where the armor was needed.
                <br />
                <b>Example:</b> "They don't make them like they used to!"
                The old buildings and appliances we still see are the ones
                that lasted. The badly made ones were torn down or thrown
                out long ago.
                <br />
                <b>Example:</b> Average returns of mutual funds look better
                than they really were if the funds that did badly and
                closed are left out of the average.
                </p>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary class="sum2">
                    Publication bias and multiple tests
                </summary>
                <p>
                Studies that find a "significant" result are much more
                likely to be published than studies that find nothing. The
                studies that found nothing stay in a file drawer.
                <br />
                This matters because a test at the 5% significance level
                will, by design, give a "significant" result about 1 time in
                20 even when there is no real effect. Run 20 tests, report
                only the one that "worked," and you have discarded the 19
                pieces of unfavorable data that put it in context.
                (See the <a href="ZTestTTest.html"><i>z</i> and <i>t</i>
                tests</a> page for what significance means.)
                </p>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary class="sum2">
                    Protecting yourself
                </summary>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                    Ask what data were collected, not just what data are
                    shown.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                    Ask why a chart starts and ends where it does.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                    Ask what happened to the cases that dropped out: the
                    failed companies, the patients who left the study, the
                    planes that did not return.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                    In your own work, decide what you will measure and how
                    you will handle outliers <em>before</em> you see the
                    results, and report everything you tested.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </details>

            <p>
            <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cherry_picking">Cherry picking</a>,
            <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Survivorship_bias">survivorship bias</a>, and
            <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Publication_bias">publication bias</a>
            on Wikipedia.
            <br />
            <a href="https://xkcd.com/882/">
                xkcd's "Significant": multiple testing in one comic.
            </a>
            </p>
        </details>

